---
title: Export Documents
sidebar:
  order: 12
---

Use `Export documents` when you need printable PDFs from NAHPU records. This is
the export for labels, tags, sheets, and other formatted documents.

## Quick way to export documents

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the `Dashboard` page.
2. Open the project menu and select `Export documents`.
3. Select a `Document Layout` profile.
4. Review or adjust page setup, template blocks, and specimen selection.
5. Optional: use the preview pane to generate a preview.
6. Add `File name`.
7. Select the save directory on desktop platforms. On Android and iOS, NAHPU
   uses the app documents directory.
8. Click `Export Documents`.

{% /steps %}

## Deep dive

Document export combines three pieces of configuration: records, document
templates, and document layouts. Records provide the data. Templates define what
appears on each label, tag, or document block. Layouts define how those template
blocks are placed on a page.

```text
Project records
    |
    v
Document template + document layout
    |
    v
Preview
    |
    v
PDF document
```

Document layouts are managed in `Settings` under `Document Presets`. A layout
can target standard page sizes such as A4, A5, or Letter, or a custom page size.
Layouts also store margins, padding, block order, rows, columns, template
counts, and page-break behavior.

When you export documents, NAHPU applies the selected layout to the selected
records and generates a PDF. Use the preview before exporting whenever the
document will be printed, because the preview helps catch text overflow,
incorrect page breaks, and unexpected template placement.

Use document layout presets for repeatable printing workflows. For example,
museum label sheets and field tag sheets should use separate layouts, even if
both use some of the same specimen data. Save the layout once, then reuse it
when exporting new records.

## Auto-fill and dynamic templates

Turn on `Auto-fill page` for a template block when labels or document blocks
should use their actual rendered height instead of a fixed number of rows. This
is useful for templates containing narrative text, nested lists, or Markdown
tables whose height changes from one record to another.

NAHPU calculates the usable page area from the selected page size and the page
padding in the document layout. Each auto-fill template keeps its configured
template height as a minimum, then grows when its visible content needs more
space. Template top and bottom padding controls the space around each template;
the next row starts immediately after the previous row's padded boundary.

Dynamic text moves later rows down so text does not overlap. NAHPU keeps a
2 mm clearance after expanded dynamic text before a genuinely lower row. Items
whose saved vertical positions are within 1 mm are treated as the same row, so
elements arranged side by side across a template stay aligned while either
element grows.

For reliable printing, use the preview with records that include the longest
expected text or largest nested lists. If a row does not fit in the remaining
usable page area, NAHPU starts it on the next page. A row taller than the usable
page area is rendered on its own page without being clipped or scaled.

Document layouts and templates are app settings. They can be shared between
devices by exporting settings or by including app settings in a database archive.
If a PDF does not look correct, edit the template or document layout preset,
regenerate the preview, and export again.

Contributors can read the storage and rendering model in [Templates and presets](../../../contributing/code/templates-and-presets)
and [Export workflows](../../../contributing/code/data-export-workflows).

Document exports are for presentation and printing. They are not a substitute
for [Export records](../export-records) when the recipient needs structured data,
and they are not a substitute for [Backup database](../#backup-database)
when you need a restorable copy of the project.
